[THOUGHT] The absence of associated cardiovascular, renal, or systemic symptoms further supports primary (essential) hypertension and non-inflammatory knee pain. The COMMON-SYMPTOM layer is now complete:  
1) Chief complaint & onset: routine BP check + occasional knee pain (insidious, intermittent);  
2) Course: waxing/waning, activity-exacerbated, rest/acetaminophen-relieved;  
3) Associated symptoms: none reported.  

Now I must pivot to the DISCRIMINATING layer — where objective data establishes diagnosis. Next highest-yield domain is **Past medical & surgical history**, as it may reveal conditions that explain both hypertension (e.g., CKD, sleep apnea, diabetes) and knee pain (e.g., prior joint injury, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, obesity-related OA). This is essential for differential refinement.

[QUERY] Do you have any diagnosed medical conditions — such as diabetes, kidney disease, thyroid problems, heart disease, arthritis, gout, or sleep apnea — or have you had any surgeries, especially involving your knees or back?

[THOUGHT] The patient cannot recall diagnosis timing — a common limitation in older adults. All eight evidence domains have now been addressed, though some with incomplete granularity:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — covered (routine BP check + intermittent knee pain);  
2) Course — covered (activity-related, improved with rest/acetaminophen);  
3) Associated symptoms — covered (none reported);  
4) Past medical history — covered (confirmed HTN and OA, no other conditions);  
5) Medications & allergies — covered (acetaminophen only, no antihypertensives, no allergies);  
6) Social/exposure — covered (no tobacco, daily exercise, age 75 — high-risk demographic);  
7) Physical exam — covered (bilateral crepitus, reduced ROM, no swelling/warmth);  
8) Lab/imaging — partially constrained (BMP and X-rays unavailable), but clinical and exam findings are highly specific for primary OA and essential hypertension.  

No further atomic, non-repetitive, high-yield questions or tests remain within the 16-turn limit. The evidence picture is complete and consistent with two coexisting, age-related, chronic conditions: essential hypertension and knee osteoarthritis. No red flags or alternative diagnoses are supported.

[FINISH]

